The source attributes the macropore/particulate-P claim to `Pease2018` and `Sharpley1995`, and the mitigation/cover-crop/edge-of-field claims to `Malone2018/Malone2014b/Malone2023`, `Christianson2013`, and `Jaynes2008`. The generated section attaches different keys to those same claims.

{"passed": false, "unfaithful": [], "miscited": ["\"a small, hydraulically active volume of macropores and cracks delivers particulate phosphorus and sediment to drains within hours, so tiles frequently dominate edge-of-field phosphorus loads \\parencite{Williams2018,King2014}\" -- the source attributes this preferential-flow/particulate-P and \"tiles frequently dominate edge-of-field phosphorus loads\" claim to \\parencite{Pease2018} (intro) and \\parencite{Pease2018,Sharpley1995} (conclusions). In the source, King2014 and Williams2018 are used only for streamflow fraction / field water export (\\parencite{King2014,Williams2018,Schilling2019}), not for the phosphorus preferential-flow claim, so these are swapped keys.", "\"cover crops clustering around $40$--$60\\%$ nitrate-load reduction, alongside controlled drainage, bioreactors, wetlands, and saturated buffers governed by residence time, temperature, and loading---so meeting downstream targets requires stacking complementary measures \\parencite{Smith2011,Gowda2012}\" -- the source attributes the 40--60% cover-crop reduction to \\parencite{Malone2018,Malone2014b,Malone2023} and the edge-of-field residence-time/temperature/loading controls to \\parencite{Christianson2013,Jaynes2008}. Smith2011 and Gowda2012 appear in the source only for the modeling/geophysics/remote-sensing toolkit (\\parencite{Smith2011,Singh2019b,Gowda2012,Allred2020}), not for mitigation practices, so these keys are swapped."]}
